{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\,\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $5,$ find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-8186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\,\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})(x+\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{36}{5}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5}) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+36 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-8186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36543778765be12f518a47d6b7e18d1a5339411c604ad31ee666063b57df1718"} {"question" "q-8186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-8186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5{x}^{2}+2x+36=0"] ["math" "5{x}^{2}+36=0"] ["math" "5{x}^{2}+6=0"] ["math" "5{x}^{2}+x-6=0"] ["math" "5{x}^{2}-36=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-8186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5{x}^{2}+36=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-28520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $-5$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-28520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-3\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=3\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3\\text{i})(x+3\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 3\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $-5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+9) & =0 \\\\ -5{x}^{2}-45 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-28520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36543778765be12f518a47d6b7e18d1a5339411c604ad31ee666063b57df1718"} {"question" "q-28520" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-28520" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5{x}^{2}+45=0"] ["math" "-5{x}^{2}+5=0"] ["math" "-5{x}^{2}+9=0"] ["math" "-5{x}^{2}-45=0"] ["math" "5{x}^{2}-9=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-28520" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5{x}^{2}-45=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-28524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $-2$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-28524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $-2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+1) & =0 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-28524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36543778765be12f518a47d6b7e18d1a5339411c604ad31ee666063b57df1718"} {"question" "q-28524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-28524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{x}^{2}+1=0"] ["math" "-2{x}^{2}+x-2=0"] ["math" "-2{x}^{2}+x-4=0"] ["math" "-2{x}^{2}-1=0"] ["math" "-2{x}^{2}-2=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-28524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2{x}^{2}-2=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-281349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a complex root $x=\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-281349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}$ is a root, we know that its complex conjugate $x=\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}$ is also a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}))(x-(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i})-x(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i})+(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x-\\cancel{3x\\text{i}}-\\sqrt{2}x+\\cancel{3x\\text{i}}+2-9{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}\\,x+2+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}\\,x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-281349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36543778765be12f518a47d6b7e18d1a5339411c604ad31ee666063b57df1718"} {"question" "q-281349" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-281349" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x^2-2\\sqrt{2}x+11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-281362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $5,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-281362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}))(x-(2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})-x(2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})+(2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})(2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-\\cancel{x\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}-2x+\\cancel{x\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}+4-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}-4x+7) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-20x+35 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e501bc1dfecb5cbf7663c98573fad717f6420b937c3a3081"} {"question" "q-281362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36543778765be12f518a47d6b7e18d1a5339411c604ad31ee666063b57df1718"} {"question" "q-281362" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032588/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4000b4c26236551e5